+# Buying-channel opportunity and governance playbook
+
+This playbook describes a reusable way to move from spend and sourcing signals to an approved buying channel, then govern adoption. It is platform-neutral; validate configuration and integration options in the target Ariba tenant before implementation.
+
+All examples and measures in this guide are generic. The companion analytics repository uses synthetic data.
+
+## 1. End-to-end opportunity map
+
+```mermaid
+flowchart TB
+    A[Organic: planned or awarded RFP pipeline] --> Q[Qualify opportunity]
+    B[Non-organic: PO spend, free text and failed searches] --> Q
+    Q --> D{Route decision}
+    D --> C[Catalogue or punchout]
+    D --> K[Contract or SOW]
+    D --> E[Quote, exception or another channel]
+    C --> I[Implement supplier and content]
+    K --> I
+    E --> G[Monthly governance]
+    I --> G
+    G --> M[Measure adoption and spend coverage]
+```
+
+**Organic** means an opportunity surfaced through a planned/active sourcing event, award or renewal. **Non-organic** means demand discovered from completed purchasing: recurring PO spend, free-text requests, failed searches, catalogue bypasses or fragmented supplier buying. These are opportunity sources; they do not, by themselves, determine the right route.
+
+## 2. Qualify before recommending a channel
+
+| Check | Evidence to capture | Decision it informs |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Scope and spend | Country, purchasing entity, commodity/L3, supplier, period, PO spend and currency | Is the demand in scope, material and comparable? |
+| Existing content | Supplier ID, active catalogue/punchout, contract, price validity, currency, UOM, ship-to/region and entity coverage | Can users already buy correctly through Ariba? |
+| Demand pattern | Line count, repeat frequency, standard specification, order value and tail of one-off demand | Can reusable catalogue content serve the need? |
+| Commercial fit | Supplier concentration, contract terms, sourcing pipeline, preferred-supplier status and local restrictions | Should the route be catalogue, contract/SOW, sourcing or another channel? |
+| Readiness and ownership | Category manager, local buyer, requester/process owner, supplier and catalogue operations contact | Who can validate and deliver the action? |
+
+Use supplier ID and commodity code as stable match keys where available. Treat supplier name, description similarity and translated text as supporting evidence; they are not sufficient alone to claim that an active catalogue covers the requested item and location.
+
+### Route logic
+
+| Recommended route | Use when | Typical next action |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Extend or use existing catalogue | Correct supplier, commodity, entity/region, item, price and UOM are active | Fix discoverability, access or requester guidance; test the buying path |
+| New catalogue or punchout | Repeatable demand with stable items/services, specifications and pricing | Confirm supplier readiness, content format, owner, refresh and support model |
+| Contract or SOW | Commercial terms or negotiated service scope matter more than line-level catalogue content | Confirm agreement coverage, buying channel, statement-of-work controls and renewal owner |
+| Quote / spot buy / free text | Variable, urgent, one-off or not yet standardized demand | Record the reason, apply delegated controls, and review recurrence |
+| Other channel / out of scope | Policy or business design points to another channel or the category is not addressable | Confirm the rationale with the category/business owner and document the denominator treatment |
+
+Avoid automatically converting all non-catalogue spend to catalogues. In media, professional services and project work, a contract/SOW or controlled sourcing route may be more suitable.
+
+## 3. Requester journey: catalogue availability and bypass control
+
+When a requester submits a non-catalogue requisition, check for an **active, applicable** catalogue match using supplier, commodity, entity/region and effective dates. If a match exists, show a soft warning with the catalogue link and let the requester review it. If the requester continues, capture a reason and send the case to the content operations queue.
+
+Suggested reasons:
+
+- Catalogue price is higher than an evidenced quote (attach the quote or reference).
+- Required item, specification or service is unavailable.
+- Delivery, location, entity or timing does not meet the need.
+- Urgent or approved exception (provide the approval/reference).
+- Other (explanation required).
+
+```mermaid
+flowchart TB
+    A[Requester starts non-catalogue PR] --> B{Applicable active catalogue match?}
+    B -->|No| C[Continue normal approval route]
+    B -->|Yes| D[Show soft warning and catalogue link]
+    D --> E{Requester uses catalogue?}
+    E -->|Yes| F[Submit through catalogue]
+    E -->|No| G[Capture reason and evidence]
+    G --> H[Catalogue Operations triage]
+    H --> I[Correct content, supplier action or approved exception]
+```
+
+Catalogue Operations should validate the reason and evidence, record an owner and due date, and route recurring issues to the category manager. Actions may include correcting price/UOM/search terms, expanding regional coverage, helping the supplier improve content, or confirming that the request is a valid exception. Repeated supplier-controlled gaps should feed a supplier improvement plan and preferred-supplier/catalogue onboarding discussion.
+
+**Ariba fit-gap:** treat the warning, reason field, required-field behavior, matching source, and event timing as design requirements. Confirm what the tenant supports through standard configuration, guided buying policies, forms or an approved extension. Do not assume a mandatory dropdown or supplier × commodity × region lookup is available without tenant validation and testing.
+
+## 4. Preferred-supplier catalogue onboarding
+
+Use the preferred-supplier list as a coverage input, not as proof that catalogue content exists. Compare preferred suppliers with active content by commodity/L3, country/entity and channel. For each gap:
+
+1. Category manager confirms strategic fit, supplier priority and intended route.
+2. Local buyer validates country/entity needs, tax, currency, delivery and any local controls.
+3. Catalogue Operations confirms the technical content model, data owner, approval and refresh process.
+4. Supplier provides validated product/service data, pricing, UOM, availability and required identifiers.
+5. Test search terms, content, price, ordering, approval and downstream PO/invoice behavior in the right environment.
+6. Publish only after business and technical sign-off; monitor first transactions, errors and user feedback.
+
+Track supplier onboarding as an action with named owner, due date, status, blocker and evidence. If the preferred supplier cannot support a catalogue, document the alternative route and escalation decision.
+
+## 5. Monthly governance journey
+
+| Participant | Monthly responsibility |
+| --- | --- |
+| Procurement analytics / operations | Refresh extracts, reconcile totals and currency, identify exceptions, prepare ranked opportunities and action register |
+| Global or regional category manager | Validate category strategy, route, supplier leverage, cross-country replication and supplier actions |
+| Country/local buyer | Confirm local entity, tax, delivery, language, policy and supplier readiness; own local implementation actions |
+| Catalogue / contract operations | Validate current content, resolve bypass cases, publish approved updates and report operational defects |
+| Ariba product owner / IT | Confirm tenant feasibility, integrations, controls, security, test approach and release/change management |
+| Business/requester representative | Validate specifications, usability, demand and reason codes from actual requesters |
+
+Recommended monthly cycle: **refresh and reconcile → review top opportunities and bypasses → agree route and owners → track delivery and blockers → check adoption and benefits at the next review.** Use an action register keyed at least by period, country/entity, commodity/L3, supplier and opportunity ID. Keep evidence, decision, owner, due date, status and next review date.
+
+## 6. Measures: keep opportunity and realized adoption separate
+
+Set the period, in-scope countries, currency conversion source/date and spend basis before comparing results. If PO spend is the agreed basis, use it consistently for both numerator and denominator. Reconcile totals to the source extract.
+
+| Measure | Suggested calculation | What it tells you |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Spend analysis coverage | Off-catalogue spend reviewed ÷ total off-catalogue spend in scope | Whether the opportunity population has been assessed |
+| Candidate opportunity coverage | Addressable off-catalogue spend with a validated catalogue/contract route ÷ total addressable off-catalogue spend | Whether the opportunity plan reaches the agreed target (for example, 75%) |
+| Realized catalogue/contract adoption | Spend transacted through approved catalogue or contract routes ÷ addressable spend | Whether implemented changes are changing buying behavior |
+| Bypass rate | Non-catalogue spend/requests with an applicable catalogue match ÷ total requests/spend with a match | Where content, price, search or user experience needs attention |
+| Content availability | Preferred-supplier × commodity × entity/region combinations with active usable content ÷ combinations in scope | Progress on onboarding and regional coverage |
+| Closure performance | Actions closed by due date ÷ actions due | Whether governance decisions lead to delivery |
+
+Keep excluded or business-led spend visible with a reason. Never count a proposed route as realized savings or adoption until transactions confirm the channel is being used.
+
+## 7. Multi-language descriptions
+
+Preserve the original requester description. Add normalized text or a reviewed translation as a separate field; never overwrite the source. Use stable IDs (supplier, commodity, entity, item and contract) first, taxonomy definitions and approved multilingual synonyms second, and translated description similarity as a candidate-matching aid only. Flag low-confidence matches for category/local-buyer review. Retain the original text and reviewer decision so the classification can be audited.
+
+## 8. Interview summary
+
+“I separate opportunity discovery from route selection. I combine the sourcing pipeline with completed PO-spend and search-gap analysis, verify existing supplier and regional content, then recommend catalogue, contract/SOW, sourcing or a controlled exception based on demand and commercial fit. At requisition time, an applicable catalogue match should prompt the requester to use it or record an evidence-backed reason. Catalogue Operations triages the reason, while category managers and local buyers review supplier coverage, onboarding, adoption and exceptions monthly. I track candidate coverage separately from actual channel adoption.”
